description: Search and select an emoji using caller-hosted Emojibase data.
---

import { PageBanner } from "/snippets/page-banner.mdx"

import { StoryEmbed } from '/snippets/ui/StoryEmbed.mdx';
import EmojiPickerReference from '/snippets/ui/generated/components/input/emoji-picker.mdx';

## Overview

`EmojiPicker` provides emoji search, category browsing, and keyboard selection. Your application supplies its labels, emoji data location, and selection handler.

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-input-emojipicker--display"
  title="Search emojis and choose a reaction"
  height={400}
/>

## Usage

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { EmojiPicker } from 'twenty-ui/components/input';
import { Text } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/typography';
import { themeCssVariables } from 'twenty-ui/theme';
import { isDefined } from 'twenty-ui/utilities';

export const EmojiPickerExample = () => {
  const [emoji, setEmoji] = useState<string | undefined>();

  return (
    <Text
      style={{
        display: 'flex',
        flexDirection: 'column',
        gap: themeCssVariables.spacing[2],
        width: 296,
      }}
    >
      <EmojiPicker
        emojibaseUrl="/emojibase"
        searchLabel="Search emojis"
        loadingLabel="Loading emojis"
        emptyLabel="No emoji found"
        onEmojiSelect={setEmoji}
      />
      <Text role="status">
        {isDefined(emoji) ? `Selected emoji: ${emoji}` : 'Choose an emoji'}
      </Text>
    </Text>
  );
};
```

The preview uses a small fixture catalogue. The usage example expects your application to serve Emojibase data at `/emojibase`.

## Data and labels

`emojibaseUrl` is required. The picker loads both `${emojibaseUrl}/${locale}/data.json` and `${emojibaseUrl}/${locale}/messages.json`. Host those files from the Emojibase data package for each supported locale. The default locale is `en`; supplying translated interface labels does not change the emoji catalogue locale.

Provide localized `searchLabel`, `loadingLabel`, and `emptyLabel`. `searchLabel` names the search field and supplies its placeholder unless `searchPlaceholder` overrides it. Emoji names, categories, and search tags come from the selected Emojibase locale.

## Selection and composition

`onEmojiSelect` receives the selected Unicode emoji string. The picker keeps its search and active emoji internally. Your application owns any selected value, reaction counts, message identifiers, permissions, persistence, and popup visibility. Compose it inside [Dropdown](/ui/components/navigation/dropdown) when it needs a popup.

The picker uses eight columns and a 280px height. Set its container width to fit the grid. Native div attributes, handlers, styles, class names, and refs target its outer container.

## Accessibility

The search input has the supplied accessible name. Focus it with Tab, or set `autoFocus` when opening a popup. Arrow keys navigate the emoji grid, and Enter selects the active emoji. Each emoji has an accessible name from the selected catalogue. `autoFocus` defaults to `false`.

## Props

<EmojiPickerReference />

<PageBanner illustration="page-cover-emoji" />

---
title: ReactionButton
description: Toggle an emoji reaction with a count and controlled pressed state.
---

import { PageBanner } from "/snippets/page-banner.mdx"

import { StoryEmbed } from '/snippets/ui/StoryEmbed.mdx';
import ReactionButtonReference from '/snippets/ui/generated/components/input/reaction-button.mdx';

## Overview

`ReactionButton` composes [Button](/ui/primitives/input/button) into a compact emoji reaction with a count. Your application supplies the pressed state, accessible name, and change handler.

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-components-reactionbutton--display"
  title="Controlled helpful reaction with a tooltip and reset action"
  height={160}
/>

## Usage

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import {
  ReactionButton,
  type ReactionButtonProps,
} from 'twenty-ui/components/input';
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';
import { Tooltip } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/surfaces';
import { Text } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/typography';
import { themeCssVariables } from 'twenty-ui/theme';

export const ReactionButtonExample = ({
  emoji = '👍',
  disabled,
  loading,
}: Partial<Pick<ReactionButtonProps, 'emoji' | 'disabled' | 'loading'>>) => {
  const [pressed, setPressed] = useState(false);
  const count = pressed ? 3 : 2;

  return (
    <Text
      style={{
        display: 'flex',
        flexDirection: 'column',
        gap: themeCssVariables.spacing[2],
      }}
    >
      <Text>Was this helpful?</Text>
      <Text
        render={<span />}
        style={{
          display: 'flex',
          alignItems: 'center',
          gap: themeCssVariables.spacing[1],
        }}
      >
        <Tooltip content={`${count} people found this helpful`}>
          <ReactionButton
            aria-label="Helpful"
            emoji={emoji}
            count={count}
            pressed={pressed}
            onPressedChange={setPressed}
            disabled={disabled}
            loading={loading}
          />
        </Tooltip>
        <Button size="sm" variant="ghost" onClick={() => setPressed(false)}>
          Reset
        </Button>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  );
};
```

## Appearance

The control is 24px tall, with rounded corners, a 16px emoji, and a small count. Pressed reactions use the theme's accent surface and text. Unpressed reactions use a transparent surface and secondary text.

`count` accepts a number or a caller-formatted string. Your application owns number formatting, abbreviation, and whether to display a reaction with a zero count.

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-components-reactionbutton--catalog"
  title="Reaction selection, counts, and unavailable states"
  height={300}
/>

## State ownership

`pressed` is required and controlled by your application. Activation calls `onPressedChange` with the requested next value. The component keeps showing the supplied `pressed` and `count` until your application updates them.

Set `loading` while a mutation is pending to show Button's loading indicator and prevent repeated activation. It preserves the supplied `pressed` value and marks the control busy. Use `disabled` when the reaction is unavailable. Selection, counts, permissions, persistence, and any optimistic updates stay in the application.

Native `onClick` runs before the change request. Calling `event.preventDefault()` in that handler cancels the request. Other native button attributes, handlers, styles, class names, and refs pass through to the button. The control always uses `type="button"`.

## Accessibility and tooltips

Provide `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` with a localized reaction name. Emoji and count are decorative and hidden from assistive technology, so include count or participant information in the supplied name or description when needed. Keep the reaction name stable as its state changes; `aria-pressed` communicates selection.

The native button supports Tab focus and activation with Enter or Space. Both `disabled` and `loading` prevent activation. The ref points to the native button.

Compose [Tooltip](/ui/primitives/surfaces/tooltip) for participant details on hover and keyboard focus, as shown above, or pass a native `title`. Tooltip content belongs to the caller and does not replace the accessible name. Long accessible labels do not enlarge the visible control.

## Props

<ReactionButtonReference />

<PageBanner illustration="page-cover-emoji" />

---
title: Button
description: Trigger actions or navigate with styled buttons.
---

import { PageBanner } from "/snippets/page-banner.mdx"

import { StoryEmbed } from '/snippets/ui/StoryEmbed.mdx';
import ButtonReference from '/snippets/ui/generated/input/button.mdx';

## Overview

Use `Button` for an action and supply its visible label as children. Use [IconButton](/ui/components/input/icon-button) for icon-only controls, with an accessible name from `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`.

For recurring design defaults, use [MainButton](/ui/components/input/main-button)
or [LightButton](/ui/components/input/light-button) from `twenty-ui/components`.
Both share Button's native props, refs, state, and rendering behavior.

<StoryEmbed
  title="Button display"
  storyId="ui-input-button-button--display"
  height={100}
/>

## Usage

```tsx
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';

export const SaveChanges = () => <Button>Save changes</Button>;
```

## Appearance

Compare variants, semantic colors, sizes, and common states separately. Each group changes one choice while keeping the others fixed.

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-input-button-button--catalog"
  title="Appearance for button"
  height={630}
/>

```tsx
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';
import { IconPlus } from 'twenty-ui/icon';

export const CreateRecord = () => (
  <Button variant="solid" color="accent" size="sm" startIcon={<IconPlus />}>
    Create record
  </Button>
);
```

Choose `solid`, `outline`, `soft`, `ghost`, or `link` independently of the `neutral`, `accent`, `danger`, or `success` color. Sizes are `sm` and `md`; the defaults are `outline`, `neutral`, and `md`. `fullWidth` fills the available width. `startIcon` and `endIcon` accept elements and are decorative. `elevated` adds a shadow and backdrop blur. Neutral outline buttons also gain a bordered surface with matching text and interaction colors. Other variants and semantic colors keep their existing palette. It defaults to `false` and does not change size or position. `shortcut` displays keyboard hints on larger screens; bind the shortcuts in your application.

Use `color="inherit"` for controls on a colored surface. The label, icons, loader, and outline border follow the surrounding text color. Interaction fills use translucent black. Outline buttons rest on a transparent surface with a 25% currentColor border and use 10% and 20% black fills on hover and press. Ghost uses transparent, 10%, and 20% black fills; soft uses 10%, 20%, and 30%. Link stays unfilled.

`inherit` supports `outline`, `ghost`, `soft`, and `link`. A resolved `solid` and `inherit` combination throws a runtime error, including defaults supplied by ButtonGroup or a wrapper. `MainButton` defaults to solid, so supply a non-solid variant when inheriting color.

```tsx
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';

export const SurfaceActions = () => (
  <>
    <Button color="inherit" variant="outline">
      Save
    </Button>
    <Button color="inherit" variant="ghost">
      Cancel
    </Button>
  </>
);
```

`link` uses compact text with no border or filled background. It follows its content height at either size, with a smaller font and narrower padding. Its neutral color uses stronger text on hover. The appearance does not determine semantics: use `onClick` for actions and `href` for navigation.

`disabled` and `loading` prevent activation. Loading keeps the label accessible and communicates a busy state. By default, `loadingPosition="center"` hides the content while preserving its layout space and centers the spinner. Set `loadingPosition` to `start` or `end` to keep children and the opposite icon visible while replacing the corresponding icon with the spinner.

Your application owns progress formatting and the translated label. Pass the complete status phrase as children, for example `Installing (42%)`.

<StoryEmbed
  title="Button loading at end"
  storyId="ui-input-button-button--loading-at-end"
  height={100}
/>

## State ownership

`Button` triggers an action and does not manage a selected value, so it has no controlled or uncontrolled selection mode. Its native `value` prop supplies form data, not selection state.

Your application owns `loading` and `disabled`, which default to `false`. Clicking a button does not change these props or automatically show loading while an asynchronous handler runs. Pass the current application state explicitly.

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';

type SaveRecordProps = { onSave: () => Promise<void> };

export const SaveRecord = ({ onSave }: SaveRecordProps) => {
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [hasError, setHasError] = useState(false);

  const handleSave = async () => {
    setLoading(true);
    setHasError(false);

    try {
      await onSave();
    } catch {
      setHasError(true);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Button loading={loading} onClick={handleSave}>
        Save record
      </Button>
      {hasError && <p role="alert">Unable to save the record. Try again.</p>}
    </>
  );
};
```

The application starts and clears loading around the save operation. The button prevents activation while loading and becomes available again when the operation finishes, including after an error. `MainButton` and `LightButton` use the same state model.

## Popup triggers

Compose a popup trigger with `Button`, `LightButton`, or another Button wrapper through `render`. The trigger supplies `data-popup-open`, so the button keeps its hover background while the popup is open and uses its active background on hover or press. Each variant and color keeps its own palette. Link backgrounds remain transparent, and disabled or loading buttons keep their disabled appearance. Buttons with `aria-pressed="true"` retain their existing toggle styling. Popup state does not set `aria-pressed` or represent a selected value.

```tsx
import { LightButton } from 'twenty-ui/components/input';
import { Popover } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/surfaces';

export const DetailsPopup = () => (
  <Popover.Root>
    <Popover.Trigger render={<LightButton>Show details</LightButton>} />
    <Popover.Portal>
      <Popover.Positioner>
        <Popover.Popup>
          <Popover.Title>Details</Popover.Title>
          <Popover.Description>
            More information about this record.
          </Popover.Description>
        </Popover.Popup>
      </Popover.Positioner>
    </Popover.Portal>
  </Popover.Root>
);
```

## Upcoming features

Your application owns feature availability and its label. Compose a disabled button with `Badge` as children so the label remains part of the button's accessible name. Supply localized text from your application.

```tsx
import { Badge } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/data-display';
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';
import { Text } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/typography';

export const UpcomingAction = () => (
  <Button disabled>
    <Text
      render={<span />}
      style={{
        display: 'inline-flex',
        alignItems: 'center',
        gap: 'var(--t-spacing-1)',
      }}
    >
      Upcoming action
      <Badge>Coming soon</Badge>
    </Text>
  </Button>
);
```

This composition also works with `MainButton` and `LightButton`. It replaces the removed `soon` and `soonLabel` props.

## Animated icons

Compose `Button` with `AnimatedIconCrossfade` to change its icon when application state changes. Pass the animated element to `startIcon` and the label as children.

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { IconPencil, IconX } from 'twenty-ui/icon';
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';
import { AnimatedIconCrossfade } from 'twenty-ui/components/layout';

export const EditActions = () => {
  const [isEditing, setIsEditing] = useState(false);

  return (
    <Button
      size="sm"
      aria-expanded={isEditing}
      onClick={() => setIsEditing(!isEditing)}
      startIcon={
        <AnimatedIconCrossfade
          isActive={isEditing}
          activeIcon={<IconX size={14} />}
          inactiveIcon={<IconPencil size={14} />}
        />
      }
    >
      Edit actions
    </Button>
  );
};
```

When an icon is itself an action, use `IconButton` or `LightIconButton` and animate its icon content. When a disclosure header owns expansion, keep its chevron decorative and expose `aria-expanded` on the header control. Keep animation styles with the feature and respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

<StoryEmbed
  title="Button with an animated icon"
  storyId="ui-components-layout-animatediconcrossfade--display"
  height={100}
/>

## Links and forms

```tsx
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';

export const DocumentationLink = () => (
  <Button
    variant="link"
    href="https://twenty.com"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noreferrer"
    hrefLang="en"
    referrerPolicy="no-referrer"
  >
    Read documentation
  </Button>
);
```

Without a custom `render`, `href` renders an anchor with link semantics, unless you explicitly set `nativeButton` to `true`. Native anchor attributes such as `download`, `hrefLang`, `ping`, and `referrerPolicy` reach the anchor. Props shared with native buttons retain their Base UI types. To supply an anchor MIME type, put it on the renderer, for example `render={<a href="/records.csv" type="text/csv">Download records</a>}` with `nativeButton={false}` and `role="link"`. Disabled and loading links cannot activate.

Use `render` to compose another element or a router link. `nativeButton` describes the rendered element and defaults to `true` for custom renderers. Set `nativeButton={false}` and `role="link"` for a navigation anchor. The anchor or router component can own its destination without a `Button` `href`.

```tsx
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';

export const RecordsLink = () => (
  <Button nativeButton={false} role="link" render={<a href="/records" />}>
    Open records
  </Button>
);
```

For a custom native `<button>`, keep `nativeButton` true. For a non-native action element such as a `<div>`, use `nativeButton={false}`; Base UI supplies button keyboard behavior. An explicit `nativeButton` or `role` is preserved even when `href` is also supplied.

Buttons default to `type="button"`. Set `type="submit"` or `type="reset"` explicitly for forms. Native attributes, event handlers, event details, and refs reach the rendered root. A ref receives the actual button, anchor, or custom root element. Keyboard handlers retain Base UI event methods, including `preventBaseUIHandler()` to cancel its built-in keyboard action. Base UI's render callback receives both root props and the current disabled state; spread the props onto your root to retain composition behavior.

## Group defaults

[ButtonGroup](/ui/primitives/input/button-group) supplies a shared variant, color, and size. An explicit prop on a child takes precedence over the group default. When neither sets a prop, the component default applies.

## Migration from ClickToActionLink

Import `Button` from `twenty-ui/primitives/input` and replace `ClickToActionLink` with `Button variant="link"`. Keep `href`, `target`, and `rel` for navigation. Actions without `href` now render a native button with keyboard activation. Native attributes, events, and refs pass through to the root.

```tsx
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';

type LogOutProps = { onLogOut: () => void };

export const LogOut = ({ onLogOut }: LogOutProps) => (
  <Button variant="link" onClick={onLogOut}>
    Log out
  </Button>
);
```

## Props

<ButtonReference />

Pass `shortcut={['Mod', 'Enter']}` for simultaneous keys or `shortcut={[['G'], ['P']]}` for successive presses. See [Shortcut](/ui/primitives/typography/shortcut) for nested combinations and the shared string formatter.

<PageBanner illustration="page-cover-pointer" />

