title: ListItemButton
description: Native action buttons with the shared list row appearance.
---

import { PageBanner } from "/snippets/page-banner.mdx"

import { StoryEmbed } from '/snippets/ui/StoryEmbed.mdx';
import ListItemButtonReference from '/snippets/ui/generated/components/navigation/list-item-button.mdx';

## Overview

Use `ListItemButton` for action rows and standalone selection buttons. It combines [ListItem](/ui/primitives/navigation/list-item) layout with a native button, including Enter and Space activation, focus, and disabled behavior. It defaults to `type="button"`.

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-components-listitembutton--display"
  title="ListItemButton example"
  height={110}
/>

## Usage

```tsx
import { ListItemButton } from 'twenty-ui/components/navigation';
import { IconCopy } from 'twenty-ui/icon';

export const DuplicateAction = ({
  onDuplicate,
}: {
  onDuplicate: () => void;
}) => (
  <ListItemButton
    startIcon={<IconCopy />}
    description="Create a copy"
    onClick={onDuplicate}
  >
    Duplicate
  </ListItemButton>
);
```

## Actions and disabled state

Pass `onClick` and `disabled` directly. Disabled buttons use the matching row appearance, prevent activation, and leave the tab order.

```tsx
import { ListItemButton } from 'twenty-ui/components/navigation';
import { IconCopy } from 'twenty-ui/icon';

type DuplicateActionProps = {
  onDuplicate: () => void;
  disabled?: boolean;
};

export const DuplicateAction = ({
  onDuplicate,
  disabled = false,
}: DuplicateActionProps) => (
  <ListItemButton
    disabled={disabled}
    onClick={onDuplicate}
    startIcon={<IconCopy />}
    description="Create a copy"
  >
    Duplicate
  </ListItemButton>
);
```

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-components-listitembutton--disabled"
  title="Disabled action row"
  height={110}
/>

Set `focusableWhenDisabled` to keep a disabled button focusable. It uses `aria-disabled` and continues to block activation. Native button attributes, events, styles, class names, and an `HTMLButtonElement` ref reach the button. Events bubble normally; callers supply any `stopPropagation()` or `preventDefault()` policy.

## Selection and visual slots

Your application owns the independent `active`, `selected`, and `focused` states. Use `active` for the open or current row. Pair `selected` with the appropriate accessible state, such as `aria-pressed` for a toggle button. `focused` highlights the row without moving DOM focus. Indicators and shortcut hints are decorative; your application registers shortcuts and handles navigation.

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { ListItemButton } from 'twenty-ui/components/navigation';

export const ShowArchivedToggle = () => {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(false);

  return (
    <ListItemButton
      aria-pressed={selected}
      selected={selected}
      indicator="check"
      onClick={() => setSelected(!selected)}
    >
      Show archived records
    </ListItemButton>
  );
};
```

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-components-listitembutton--selected"
  title="Selected action row"
  height={110}
/>

Use `startIcon`, `endIcon`, `description`, and `descriptionPlacement` for supporting content. `color="danger"` marks a destructive action. `shortcutJoinLabel` controls the text between sequential shortcuts, and `hasSubmenu` displays a submenu indicator.

## Multiline supporting content

Set `descriptionPlacement="below"` to wrap supporting text beneath the label. The label retains its truncation behavior. Pass [Text](/ui/primitives/typography/text) with `truncate` or `lineClamp` as `description` when you need a shorter preview. The `inline` and `end` placements keep their existing appearance.

```tsx
import { ListItemButton } from 'twenty-ui/components/navigation';
import { Avatar } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/data-display';

type PlanningActionProps = {
  active?: boolean;
  disabled?: boolean;
  onOpen: () => void;
};

export const PlanningAction = ({
  active = false,
  disabled = false,
  onOpen,
}: PlanningActionProps) => (
  <ListItemButton
    active={active}
    disabled={disabled}
    descriptionPlacement="below"
    description="Review the proposed timeline and confirm the next steps with the team."
    startIcon={<Avatar name="Alex Morgan" size="md" imageProps={{ alt: '' }} />}
    onClick={onOpen}
  >
    Quarterly planning
  </ListItemButton>
);
```

`active` applies the current-row background. Set `selectionAppearance="accent"` for an accent fill on selected rows with any description placement; the default preserves the existing indicator-based selection styling. `focused` applies the shared highlighted background; the focus-visible outline follows actual keyboard focus. These props do not add accessible state or move focus. Native button activation and disabled behavior remain unchanged.

## Independent controls

Keep independent buttons and checkboxes beside `ListItemButton` so each retains its own focus and activation. Do not nest them in its content or icon slots. The component has no `actions` slot. Use a `ListItem` with its default `div` root for [a row with independent controls](/ui/primitives/navigation/list-item#independent-controls), and supply disabled behavior and event propagation on each control.

```tsx
import { ListItemButton } from 'twenty-ui/components/navigation';
import { Button } from 'twenty-ui/primitives/input';

type ContactActionsProps = {
  onOpen: () => void;
  onDetails: () => void;
};

export const ContactActions = ({ onOpen, onDetails }: ContactActionsProps) => (
  <div style={{ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: 8 }}>
    <ListItemButton onClick={onOpen} description="Account owner">
      Alex Morgan
    </ListItemButton>
    <Button onClick={onDetails} aria-label="Open Alex Morgan's details">
      Details
    </Button>
  </div>
);
```

<StoryEmbed
  storyId="ui-components-listitembutton--independent-action"
  title="Sibling action controls"
  height={110}
/>

Use `ListItem` for passive content, links, or custom semantic owners. Use the existing [Dropdown](/ui/components/navigation/dropdown), and [Select](/ui/primitives/input/select) item parts within their popup owners, which supply roles and keyboard navigation.

